00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:02.340
MINHAZ KAZI: Welcome
to DataVis DevTalk.

00:00:02.340 --> 00:00:05.130
I'm your host, Minhaz
Kazi, developer advocate

00:00:05.130 --> 00:00:06.870
for Data Studio.

00:00:06.870 --> 00:00:09.660
In this video, we'll talk about
the Apps Script development

00:00:09.660 --> 00:00:13.050
environment that you will use
to create your own Community

00:00:13.050 --> 00:00:14.010
Connector.

00:00:14.010 --> 00:00:14.820
Let's get started.

00:00:14.820 --> 00:00:18.660
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:20.580 --> 00:00:23.550
If you're not already familiar
with Google Apps Script,

00:00:23.550 --> 00:00:26.400
its development environment may
seem quite different from what

00:00:26.400 --> 00:00:27.690
you're used to.

00:00:27.690 --> 00:00:29.880
When you use Apps Script,
you develop your code

00:00:29.880 --> 00:00:32.369
in a browser-based code editor.

00:00:32.369 --> 00:00:34.560
The code is a specialized
version of JavaScript,

00:00:34.560 --> 00:00:36.060
and you don't have
to host your app,

00:00:36.060 --> 00:00:39.560
as it lives and runs on
Google servers in the cloud.

00:00:39.560 --> 00:00:41.310
We're not going to go
into too much detail

00:00:41.310 --> 00:00:43.240
about App Script in this video.

00:00:43.240 --> 00:00:45.810
We'll cover just the basics to
get you started with building

00:00:45.810 --> 00:00:47.760
your own Community Connector.

00:00:47.760 --> 00:00:50.100
If you're interested to
learn more about Apps Script,

00:00:50.100 --> 00:00:52.080
you can check out the
Apps Script Codelab.

00:00:52.080 --> 00:00:54.170
The link is in the
description below.

00:00:54.170 --> 00:00:55.944
To start building
your own connector,

00:00:55.944 --> 00:00:57.610
head over to the Apps
Script development

00:00:57.610 --> 00:01:01.440
enrollment at script.google.com,
and start a new project.

00:01:01.440 --> 00:01:03.540
An Apps Script project
represents a collection

00:01:03.540 --> 00:01:06.000
of files and resources,
sometimes referred

00:01:06.000 --> 00:01:08.760
to simply as a script.

00:01:08.760 --> 00:01:10.650
In our video
series, we might use

00:01:10.650 --> 00:01:12.750
Apps Script project,
connector code,

00:01:12.750 --> 00:01:16.020
and script interchangeably.

00:01:16.020 --> 00:01:19.470
Your project can contain one
or multiple JavaScript files.

00:01:19.470 --> 00:01:21.935
The project will also
contain a manifest,

00:01:21.935 --> 00:01:24.960
a special JSON file that
contains certain metadata

00:01:24.960 --> 00:01:27.340
about the script itself.

00:01:27.340 --> 00:01:30.750
We'll talk more about the
manifest in a later video.

00:01:30.750 --> 00:01:34.270
Apps Script provides services
to help you develop your app,

00:01:34.270 --> 00:01:37.800
including cache service, lock
service, property service,

00:01:37.800 --> 00:01:39.430
and many other.

00:01:39.430 --> 00:01:41.100
For example, the
cache service allows

00:01:41.100 --> 00:01:45.060
you to temporarily cache results
for a time-consuming operation.

00:01:45.060 --> 00:01:47.730
The service lets you cache
data at public level,

00:01:47.730 --> 00:01:51.210
where the data can be accessed
by all users of your connector,

00:01:51.210 --> 00:01:53.910
or at private level, where
the data can be accessed only

00:01:53.910 --> 00:01:56.430
for individual users.

00:01:56.430 --> 00:01:58.770
There is even a
DataStudioApp service

00:01:58.770 --> 00:02:01.500
that helps you to build your
own Community Connector.

00:02:01.500 --> 00:02:04.410
You can write your
code, execute it, test,

00:02:04.410 --> 00:02:06.750
and debug all within the
Apps Script environment

00:02:06.750 --> 00:02:08.889
without leaving your browser.

00:02:08.889 --> 00:02:11.460
However, if you want to
use your own favorite IDE

00:02:11.460 --> 00:02:14.430
to develop your code,
that's also possible.

00:02:14.430 --> 00:02:16.870
Then you'll need to use Clasp.

00:02:16.870 --> 00:02:19.980
Clasp is an open source tool
separate from the Apps Script

00:02:19.980 --> 00:02:21.360
platform.

00:02:21.360 --> 00:02:24.030
It lets you develop and
manage Apps Script project

00:02:24.030 --> 00:02:26.480
locally from your terminal
rather than the Apps Script's

00:02:26.480 --> 00:02:27.870
editor.

00:02:27.870 --> 00:02:30.810
You can also use your favorite
developing tools, like Git,

00:02:30.810 --> 00:02:33.180
when building your
connector with Clasp.

00:02:33.180 --> 00:02:37.470
The Clasp Codelab provides a
overview of all Clasp features.

00:02:37.470 --> 00:02:40.130
The link is in the description
if you want to try it out.

00:02:40.130 --> 00:02:42.120
Now, let's say you have
built your connector.

00:02:42.120 --> 00:02:44.610
When you or some other
user tries to use it,

00:02:44.610 --> 00:02:46.800
Data Studio will execute
the connector code

00:02:46.800 --> 00:02:48.810
on the user's behalf.

00:02:48.810 --> 00:02:51.600
When a user uses a connector
for the first time,

00:02:51.600 --> 00:02:53.040
they must authorize
the connector

00:02:53.040 --> 00:02:55.020
to act on their behalf.

00:02:55.020 --> 00:02:56.730
During this time,
the UI presents

00:02:56.730 --> 00:02:59.200
a prompt to the user to
start the authorization

00:02:59.200 --> 00:03:00.960
flow for that connector.

00:03:00.960 --> 00:03:04.530
During this flow, the UI tells
the user what the connector

00:03:04.530 --> 00:03:06.480
wants permission to do.

00:03:06.480 --> 00:03:10.260
For example, if the connector
is fetching data from BigQuery,

00:03:10.260 --> 00:03:13.800
it will ask for BigQuery's
scope during the authorization.

00:03:13.800 --> 00:03:15.660
You can read more
about the Oauth process

00:03:15.660 --> 00:03:18.420
and the Oauth scopes at the
link in the description.

00:03:18.420 --> 00:03:20.220
This overview of
Apps Script should

00:03:20.220 --> 00:03:23.160
help you to get started with
building your own connector.

00:03:23.160 --> 00:03:24.810
You can always go
to a developer site

00:03:24.810 --> 00:03:27.690
at
developers.google.com/datastudio

00:03:27.690 --> 00:03:29.340
for more information.

00:03:29.340 --> 00:03:31.950
If you liked this video,
stay tuned on the GCP channel

00:03:31.950 --> 00:03:33.577
for more Data Studio content.

00:03:33.577 --> 00:03:34.410
Thanks for watching.

00:03:34.410 --> 00:03:37.998
